@*
    OdsImageCropDialog — the product's ONE client-side crop + downscale dialog
    (Odyssey Design System · Components · ImageCropDialog). Extracted from
    ContactAvatarDialog when the user profile picture arrived (issue #94 §5): one cropper,
    two surfaces — a contact's image or logo, and the signed-in user's own picture.

    It COMPOSES OdsFormDialog rather than hand-rolling role="dialog", focus trapping and focus return:
    the modal shell already owns all three, so Escape closes the dialog and returns focus to the
    invoking control for free, and there is no keyboard trap to get wrong twice.

    The controls are native <input type="range"> — Zoom, Horizontal, Vertical — each with a visible,
    programmatically associated <label>, an aria-valuetext that reads as a percentage, and arrow /
    Home / End support BY CONSTRUCTION. That is why the widget is built from range inputs rather than a
    custom draggable surface: pointer drag on the canvas is an ADDITIONAL affordance, never the only
    one.

    EVERY ELEMENT ID IS GENERATED PER INSTANCE. The extracted dialog can be mounted twice on one page,
    and the hardcoded ids this grew from (`cav-zoom`, `cav-x`, `cav-y`, `cav-file`) would break every
    <label for> and aria-describedby the moment that happened — silently defeating the two
    accessibility contracts below rather than failing loudly.

    The <canvas> is aria-hidden and the preview state is described in text beside it. A canvas exposes
    no accessible structure at all, and the three ranges already carry the state.

    ONE VALIDATION-FAILURE RULE:
      · A failure attributable to a CONTROL (a rejected file) sets aria-invalid on that control, links
        the message with aria-describedby and MOVES FOCUS to it. It is not role="alert" — focusing a
        control whose description is the error already announces it, and adding the role announces it
        twice.
      · A failure NOT attributable to a control (rejected by the server, network) goes to a role="alert"
        region and focus does not move. Its text is the server's own ProblemDetails message, which names
        the actual limit, rather than a generic client string.

    NO NUMBER IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE. The stored cap comes from IUploadLimitsCache tightened by the
    caller's SurfaceMegabytes — min(instance cap, surface cap), never max — and the source caps arrive
    as parameters from the surface's own limits class. A literal would go stale the moment an
    administrator changed the setting.

    THE UPLOAD IS A DELEGATE, not an injected resource client: the two surfaces POST to different
    endpoints and this component under Components/ must not know either. But it KEEPS
    @inject IUploadLimitsCache and resolves the cap itself, because it is the file that constructs the
    ApiUpload — and UploadCapSourceTests' "reads a live cap" lint keys on exactly that pairing.
*@

@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IUploadLimitsCache UploadLimits

<OdsFormDialog Open="Open" OpenChanged="OpenChanged"
               Icon="crop"
               Wide="true"
               RequiredLegend="false"
               Title="@ResolvedTitle"
               Subtitle="@Subtitle"
               SubmitLabel="Save"
               SubmitIcon="check"
               OnSubmit="SaveAsync"
               OnSaved="OnSaved">
    <div class="odc-crop-body" aria-busy="@(_saving ? "true" : null)">
        @* Not attributable to any control the user can correct — announced where it is, focus stays. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_serverError))
        {
            <div class="odc-crop-alert" role="alert">
                <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:18px;">error_outline</span>
                <span>@_serverError</span>
            </div>
        }

        @* The shared upload field, single-file and kind-less: one image per subject, and the file's
           KIND is not the user's to set here — what gets stored is generated. *@
        <OdsFieldShell Label="@(_hasImage ? "Source image" : ChooseLabel)">
            <div @ref="_dropzoneHost">
                <OdsFileUpload Id="@FileId"
                               Compact="true"
                               Multiple="false"
                               ShowKinds="false"
                               Accept="@AcceptAttribute"
                               Files="_files"
                               FilesChanged="OnFilesChangedAsync"
                               Error="@_fileError"
                               Hint="@SourceHint" />
            </div>
        </OdsFieldShell>

        <div class="odc-crop-stage">
            @* Pointer drag is an addition to the ranges. The frame carries no tab stop and no role:
               everything it offers is reachable from the three controls beside it. *@
            <div class="odc-crop-frame @(Cover ? "round" : "ground")"
                 @onpointerdown="OnPointerDown" @onpointermove="OnPointerMove"
                 @onpointerup="OnPointerUp" @onpointerleave="OnPointerUp">
                <canvas @ref="_canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
                @if (!_hasImage)
                {
                    <span class="odc-crop-frame-empty" aria-hidden="true">
                        <span class="material-icons" style="font-size:30px;">@EmptyIcon</span>
                    </span>
                }
            </div>

            <div class="odc-crop-controls">
                @* The canvas is aria-hidden, so this sentence IS the preview for anyone not looking
                   at it. *@
                <p class="odc-crop-state">@StateText</p>

                <label class="odc-crop-ctl" for="@ZoomId">
                    <span class="odc-crop-ctl-l">Zoom<b>@Percent(_zoom)</b></span>
                    <input id="@ZoomId" type="range" class="odc-range" min="100" max="300" step="5"
                           value="@_zoom" disabled="@(!_hasImage)"
                           aria-valuetext="@Percent(_zoom)"
                           @oninput="@(e => SetZoom(e.Value?.ToString()))" />
                </label>

                <label class="odc-crop-ctl" for="@OffsetXId">
                    <span class="odc-crop-ctl-l">Horizontal<b>@HorizontalText</b></span>
                    <input id="@OffsetXId" type="range" class="odc-range" min="-100" max="100" step="2"
                           value="@_offsetX" disabled="@(!_hasImage)"
                           aria-valuetext="@HorizontalText"
                           @oninput="@(e => SetOffsetX(e.Value?.ToString()))" />
                </label>

                <label class="odc-crop-ctl" for="@OffsetYId">
                    <span class="odc-crop-ctl-l">Vertical<b>@VerticalText</b></span>
                    <input id="@OffsetYId" type="range" class="odc-range" min="-100" max="100" step="2"
                           value="@_offsetY" disabled="@(!_hasImage)"
                           aria-valuetext="@VerticalText"
                           @oninput="@(e => SetOffsetY(e.Value?.ToString()))" />
                </label>

                <div class="odc-crop-reset">
                    <OdsButton Variant="OdsButtonVariant.Text" Icon="restart_alt" OnClick="ResetAsync">Reset</OdsButton>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* Says what actually happens to the file, because the server strip is not otherwise visible. *@
        <p class="odc-crop-note">@Note</p>

        @* The page's own polite region is elsewhere; the dialog carries its own because its async
           states begin and end inside it. *@
        <OdsLiveAnnouncer Message="@_live" />
    </div>
</OdsFormDialog>
